※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A value of a sum, and the `match` that takes it apart.
※
※ **A sum holds exactly one of its parts**, so a value of one says which part it
※ is: `Number(.whole ← 5i64)` is the record shape with one part written, which
※ is where a product and a sum differ said in the one place a reader is looking.
※
※ **What a value of one is, is where its bytes are.**  A sum is its largest part
※ with a tag after it -- the tag last, since a tag ahead of a part wanting eight
※ bytes is seven bytes of padding and behind it is often none -- and that is not
※ a thing a register holds.  So the value is room in the frame, the part written
※ at its start and the tag after it, and what travels is where that room is.
※
※ **An arm names the type of the part it takes**, which is why no two parts of a
※ sum may have one type: an arm could not choose between them.  The chain of
※ comparisons is over the tag, which is what an enumeration is taken apart with
※ and for the same reason -- what a value holds is a number, and which arm runs
※ follows from it.
※
※ **A part that carries nothing** is written `.name ← true`: something has to
※ stand where a value is written, and what it says is that this is the part.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, whose `enum` this is and whose `match` is this match, with
※ the difference that a variant there is named and here it is named *and* typed;
※ **Haskell**, whose `data` declaration separates the alternatives with the same
※ `|`; **C**, whose union carries no tag at all and where keeping the two in step
※ is the program''s business; **Zig**, whose `union(enum)` is this with the tag
※ written out as an enumeration of its own.

type Point = x : u8 ; y : u8
type Shape = dot : Point | line : u32 | empty : void

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let a: Shape = Shape(.dot ← Point(.x ← 2u8, .y ← 3u8))
    if size(a) ≠ 5u32:
        1u6
    else:
        rest()

@[impure]
fn rest() → u6:
    ※ a part that is a number, and one that carries nothing
    let b: Shape = Shape(.line ← 7u32)
    let c: Shape = Shape(.empty ← true)
    if size(b) ≠ 7u32:
        2u6
    elif size(c) ≠ 0u32:
        3u6
    ※ and an arm that takes every part no earlier arm took
    elif whichever(c) ≠ 1u8:
        4u6
    elif whichever(b) ≠ 0u8:
        5u6
    else:
        0u6

※ A match whose arms bind what each part carries.
fn size(s: Shape) → u32:
    match s:
        Point(p):
            ⎕widen(p.x, ⌜u32⌝) + ⎕widen(p.y, ⌜u32⌝)
        u32(n):
            n
        void:
            0u32

※ And one whose wildcard takes the rest, binding nothing.
fn whichever(s: Shape) → u8:
    match s:
        u32:
            0u8
        _:
            1u8
